{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Final Project and Professional Vision","title":"Module 6 Final Studio","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting module 6 final studio to Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"06","section":"08","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_final_project_and_professional_vision","cat531_supplement_module_6_final_studio","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/06/07"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_6_8_module_6_final_studio_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_6_8_module_6_final_studio_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_6_8_module_6_final_studio_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_6_8_module_6_final_studio_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":8,"top_tier_hardened":false},"content":"# Module 6 Final Studio\n\n![Module 6 Final Studio textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/06-08-module-6-final-studio.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nThe capstone studio asks for something harder than four good artifacts; it asks for four artifacts that form one chain of judgment. A tension map that decides equity should govern, an implementation plan that quietly drops the equity fallback, a family memo that promises a safeguard the plan abandoned, and a reflection that never checks whether the safeguard worked, are four competent documents that contradict each other. Integration is the skill of making each artifact follow from the prior one, so that a reviewer reading the set sees a single coherent professional acting, not four assignments stapled together.\n\nThis is also where intellectual honesty gets tested under pressure. The temptation in a capstone is to smooth every artifact into a confident yes, resolving every tension and hiding every cost so the package looks finished. A stronger capstone keeps at least one hard tradeoff visible and shows how you reasoned through it, because the existence of an unresolved tension, handled openly, is more credible than a suspiciously frictionless story. The Design Tension Studio map is the keystone: its decision should be traceable into every other artifact in the set.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA candidate submits the four Module 6 artifacts in order: tension map, implementation plan, family memo, evidence-based reflection, each on a pilot of an AI feedback tool. A reviewer reads them and writes one note: \"These do not talk to each other.\" The map decided that equity should win, so the tool would be paired with a device fallback. But the implementation plan never schedules the fallback, the memo discloses a safeguard the plan does not actually deliver, and the reflection reports a class average that hides whether the fallback group kept pace. Each artifact is individually fine; together they describe a teacher who decided one thing and did another.\n\nThe failure is not in any single document but in the seams between them. A capstone is judged on coherence, and coherence lives precisely where one artifact must honor a commitment another artifact made.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nIntegrating a capstone means building a traceable chain where each artifact discharges the obligations created by the previous one:\n\n- **A decision that propagates.** The governing value chosen in the tension map (say, equity over efficiency) must visibly shape the plan, the memo, and the reflection. If equity won on paper but vanishes in the plan, the decision was never real.\n- **Consistency across artifacts.** No later artifact may contradict an earlier commitment. A plan that drops the fallback the map required, or a memo that promises what the plan omits, breaks the chain and signals that the documents were written in isolation.\n- **A preserved tradeoff.** Rather than resolving every tension into a clean yes, a strong capstone keeps one real, unresolved cost visible (the fallback consumes class time) and shows the reasoning that accepted it. Visible tradeoffs read as honesty; their absence reads as concealment.\n- **A reflection that closes the loop.** The final reflection should test the very commitment the map made, with evidence, so the chain ends where it began rather than drifting to an unrelated conclusion.\n\nThis is synthesis applied to your own body of work: the same discipline that resolved competing values in Module 6 now must hold across four documents instead of within one. The course anchors audit the chain. [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge) checks that the through-line still serves a learning target; critical edtech studies (Selwyn) checks that the equity commitment survives into the plan and reflection rather than being announced and abandoned; datafication (Perrotta & Selwyn, 2019) checks that the memo's promises match what the tool actually does. A capstone where the artifacts agree only because each avoided the hard part is not integrated; it is merely uniform.\n\n<inline-check question=\"Your tension map decides equity governs, so the tool will be paired with a device fallback. Your implementation plan never schedules that fallback. Which integration property has broken?\" explanation=\"Consistency across artifacts has broken: a later artifact (the plan) drops a commitment an earlier one (the map) made. If equity won on paper but vanishes in the plan, the decision was never real, and the chain signals the documents were written in isolation.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Per-artifact polish\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Consistency: the plan contradicts the map governing decision\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The reflection has too much data\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The memo is too detailed\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nOrder the four capstone artifacts so each one discharges the obligation the previous one created:\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Tension map: decide the governing value (equity over efficiency) and require a device fallback\",\"Implementation plan: schedule the fallback the map required, with owners and gates\",\"Family memo: disclose exactly the safeguard the plan implements, promising nothing it omits\",\"Evidence-based reflection: test whether the equity commitment held, using subgroup data\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nThe weak version below sounds plausible, but it does not yet give a reader enough evidence:\n\n> I made the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision clearer.\n\nThat claim is too thin because \"clearer\" does not identify the audience, the problem, or the evidence. A stronger claim would read:\n\n> I revised the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision so that the intended audience can see the decision, the constraint, and the evidence source. The revision is justified by **critical edtech studies**, and I can point to the exact part of the artifact that changed.\n\nThe improvement is not longer wording. It is a better trace. The reader can now see the artifact, the constraint from **critical edtech studies**, and the exact revision that followed.\n\n### Worked Example: An Annotated Capstone Integration Decision\n\nFollow a teacher assembling the four Module 6 artifacts into one integrated final studio capstone.\n\n1. **The four pieces.** Tension map (efficiency versus equity), implementation plan, family memo, and evidence-based reflection on a pilot of an AI feedback tool.\n2. **Before (stapled).** First submission orders the four files but each was written on its own. A reviewer annotates: \"These do not talk to each other.\"\n3. **Trace the chain.** Revision makes the tension map's decision (equity wins, so keep a device fallback) drive the plan, which schedules the fallback, which the family memo discloses, which the reflection then tests with subgroup data.\n4. **Fix the contradiction.** The reviewer flags that the plan had quietly dropped the fallback. The teacher restores it so a later artifact no longer contradicts the earlier value commitment.\n5. **Keep a visible tradeoff (after).** Rather than smoothing every artifact to a confident yes, the capstone keeps one unresolved tension (the fallback costs class time) and shows how the teacher reasoned through it.\n\nThe before is a folder; the after is one chain of judgment where each artifact follows from the prior decision and the hard tradeoff stays visible.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Repairing the broken seam in your capstone\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"A reviewer reads your four Module 6 artifacts on an AI-feedback pilot and writes one note: These do not talk to each other. Your map decided equity governs (keep a device fallback), but the plan never schedules the fallback, the memo discloses it anyway, and the reflection reports only a class average. Where do you start?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Polish each document so all four read more professionally\",\"next\":\"polish\",\"feedback\":\"Per-artifact polish is the last layer. The failure is in the seams between documents, not within any one.\"},{\"label\":\"Trace the keystone decision (equity wins) through every artifact and find where it disappears\",\"next\":\"trace\",\"feedback\":\"Right: coherence lives precisely where one artifact must honor a commitment another made.\"},{\"label\":\"Drop the equity commitment so nothing contradicts it\",\"next\":\"drop\",\"feedback\":\"Abandoning the keystone to remove the contradiction guts the decision the capstone is built on.\"}]},\"polish\":{\"prompt\":\"You polish all four, but the reviewer note stands: the plan still omits the fallback the map and memo promise. What did polish fail to fix?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"The broken seam: a later artifact contradicts an earlier commitment\",\"next\":\"trace\",\"feedback\":\"Exactly. Coherence across artifacts outranks the finish of any single one.\"}]},\"drop\":{\"prompt\":\"With equity dropped, the artifacts no longer contradict, but the capstone now decides nothing meaningful. Is uniformity the same as integration?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"No; artifacts that agree only by avoiding the hard part are merely uniform\",\"next\":\"trace\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Restore the keystone and make it propagate instead.\"}]},\"trace\":{\"prompt\":\"Tracing the chain, you confirm the plan quietly dropped the device fallback the map required. What is the repair?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Restore the fallback in the plan so a later artifact no longer contradicts the value commitment\",\"next\":\"tradeoff\",\"feedback\":\"Correct: the governing value must be findable, by name, in the plan, the memo, and the reflection.\"},{\"label\":\"Edit the memo to stop promising the fallback\",\"next\":\"weaken\",\"feedback\":\"This resolves the contradiction by abandoning the equity safeguard, which weakens the very decision the map made.\"}]},\"weaken\":{\"prompt\":\"Removing the safeguard from the memo means the equity decision survives nowhere downstream. What does an integrated capstone require instead?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Make the decision propagate: restore the fallback in the plan, not delete it from the memo\",\"next\":\"tradeoff\",\"feedback\":\"Right. A decision that propagates is the first property of integration.\"}]},\"tradeoff\":{\"prompt\":\"The chain now holds: map to plan to memo to reflection. But the fallback consumes class time, a real cost. Should you smooth this away to make the capstone look confident?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Yes; a capstone should present total confidence\",\"next\":\"oversmooth\",\"feedback\":\"Over-smoothing makes an experienced reviewer distrust the package. Real teaching involves accepted tradeoffs.\"},{\"label\":\"No; keep the class-time cost visible and show why equity was judged worth it\",\"next\":\"honest\",\"feedback\":\"Visible tradeoffs read as honesty; their absence reads as concealment.\"}]},\"oversmooth\":{\"prompt\":\"A reviewer reads a frictionless capstone with no visible cost. Why does it lower rather than raise their trust?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"A capstone showing no tradeoff has either avoided the hard decisions or hidden them\",\"next\":\"honest\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Keep one real, reasoned tradeoff visible.\"}]},\"honest\":{\"prompt\":\"Your capstone now traces one decision through all four artifacts with the class-time cost acknowledged throughout. What makes it integrated rather than a collection?\",\"feedback\":\"A reviewer can follow one teacher single decision from value to plan to disclosure to evidence without finding a broken seam, and the reflection closes the loop by testing the original equity commitment with subgroup data rather than a hiding average. The honest tradeoff stays on the page because hiding it would make the whole chain less trustworthy, not more. That propagation, consistency, and visible honesty are what turn four competent documents into one coherent professional acting.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nTrace the keystone decision through every artifact before you polish any of them. The governing value from the tension map should be findable, by name, in the plan, the memo, and the reflection; wherever it disappears, you have a broken seam to repair. Coherence across artifacts outranks the finish of any single one.\n\nCheck the assembled capstone against three questions:\n\n1. **Propagation check:** Does the tension map's governing decision visibly shape every other artifact?\n2. **Consistency check:** Does any later artifact contradict an earlier commitment, such as a fallback promised then dropped?\n3. **Honesty check:** Is at least one real tradeoff kept visible and reasoned through, rather than smoothed into a frictionless yes?\n\nIf any answer is missing, the capstone is a collection rather than an integration.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe dominant error is the stapled portfolio: four artifacts written in isolation and ordered in a folder, which looks complete and falls apart the moment a reviewer reads across the seams. A related error is the silent contradiction, where a later artifact quietly abandons a commitment an earlier one made, usually because the teacher solved each document's local problem without rechecking the chain.\n\nThe subtlest and most costly misreading is over-smoothing. Believing a capstone should present total confidence, candidates resolve every tension and hide every cost, producing a package so frictionless that an experienced reviewer distrusts it. Real teaching involves accepted tradeoffs; a capstone that shows none has either avoided the hard decisions or concealed them, and either reading undercuts the very judgment the studio is meant to demonstrate.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nA teacher's capstone centers on adopting an AI tutoring tool. The tension map decides that data privacy governs over convenience, so the tool will run only on school devices with a no-retention setting. Trace that keystone forward. The implementation plan must then schedule device access and verify the no-retention configuration as a gated prerequisite, not an afterthought; if the plan assumes home use to save class time, it has contradicted the map. The family memo must disclose exactly the no-retention practice the plan implements, with no promise the plan cannot keep. The reflection must then report whether the no-retention, school-only constraint actually held in practice and what it cost. (These are statutory decisions, not just good practice: the no-retention configuration maps to COPPA's amended rule, which bars indefinite retention of children's data and requires a written retention policy, effective June 23, 2025 with compliance by April 22, 2026; the school-only constraint and the accurate family memo map to FERPA's education-record and consent regime, where the vendor operates under the \"school official\" exception only if it does not retain or repurpose data beyond the contracted educational service. The FTC declined to codify school-authorized consent in the 2025 amendment, so the COPPA-school link is enforced under existing FTC guidance pending FERPA rulemaking.)\n\nNow keep the tradeoff visible. The privacy decision costs flexibility: students cannot use the tutor at home, so some practice time is lost. A smoothed capstone would bury this; the integrated one states it, explains why privacy was judged worth the lost practice, and the reflection checks whether the lost practice hurt outcomes. A reviewer reading all four can follow one teacher's single decision from value to plan to disclosure to evidence, with the cost acknowledged throughout, which is exactly what an integrated professional vision looks like.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read a capstone, do not read the artifacts in isolation; read across them, tracking the keystone decision from the map into each later document and flagging every place it weakens, vanishes, or is contradicted. Then look for the tradeoff: a capstone with no visible cost has probably hidden one. By the end of CAT 531 06.08 you should be able to read any multi-artifact submission and locate its weakest seam, the point where one document fails to honor what another committed to.\n\nA strong reader of an integrated capstone can answer four questions:\n\n1. Is the tension map's governing decision traceable into every other artifact by name?\n2. Where, if anywhere, does a later artifact contradict an earlier commitment?\n3. What real tradeoff is kept visible, and is the reasoning that accepted it shown?\n4. Does the final reflection test the original decision with evidence, closing the loop?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA capstone is strong when a reviewer can trace one decision through all four artifacts without finding a broken seam, and when at least one honest tradeoff is visible and reasoned. Per-artifact polish is the last layer; the load-bearing layers are propagation, consistency, and visible honesty. If a seam breaks (a fallback promised then dropped), mark it and ask BigAL to compare the stapled version with the integrated version of that specific link rather than to \"improve the capstone overall.\"\n\nA useful closing line would read: \"I restored the device fallback in the plan because my map decided equity governs, and a capstone that announces equity then drops its only safeguard is not integrated; I kept the class-time cost of the fallback visible because hiding the tradeoff would make the whole chain less trustworthy, not more.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the capstone discipline from this course to a school improvement proposal you might write as a practicing teacher: a problem statement, an action plan, a stakeholder communication, and an evaluation design. The artifacts differ, but the integration demand is identical: the value your problem statement commits to must propagate into the plan, survive into the communication, and be tested by the evaluation, with the real tradeoff kept visible. If you understood the capstone as a chain of judgment rather than a set of deliverables, you build coherent proposals for the rest of your career. If you only assembled four Module 6 files, the first real-world proposal with mismatched seams will read exactly as the stapled capstone did.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/06-08-module-6-final-studio-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"06.08\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision for CAT 531 06.08. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_module_6_final_studio\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision captures instructional purpose, equity or privacy boundary, support request, revision rationale, and confidence change.\n\n## References\n\n- Mishra, P., & Koehler, M. J. (2006). Technological pedagogical content knowledge: A framework for teacher knowledge. Teachers College Record. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- Selwyn, N. (2016). Is technology good for education? Polity Press. ISBN 9780745696461.\n- Perrotta, C., & Selwyn, N. (2019). Deep learning goes to school: Toward a relational understanding of AI in education. Learning, Media and Technology. https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017\n- Ning, Y., et al. (2024). Teachers' AI-TPACK: Exploring the relationship between knowledge elements. Sustainability, 16(3). https://doi.org/10.3390/su16030978\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n- Federal Trade Commission (2025). Children's Online Privacy Protection Rule: Final amended rule (16 CFR Part 312). https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/04/22/2025-05904/childrens-online-privacy-protection-rule\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_8_module_6_final_studio_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_module_6_final_studio","stem":"In the Module 6 final studio, a teacher assembles the capstone: a tension map, an implementation plan, a family memo, and a reflection. What turns these from a stapled folder into an integrated capstone?","options":["Putting them in the order they were assigned.","Showing that the value decision in the tension map drives the implementation plan, which the family memo discloses, which the reflection then tests with evidence.","Making all four artifacts the same visual length.","Adding a cover page that lists all four artifacts."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"An integrated capstone shows the artifacts as one chain of judgment, where each piece follows from the prior decision.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_6_8_module_6_final_studio_stapled_not_integrated","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_final_project_and_professional_vision","cat531_supplement_module_6_final_studio","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_8_module_6_final_studio_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_module_6_final_studio","stem":"A reviewer annotates the capstone: \"Your tension map prioritizes equity, but your implementation plan quietly drops the device fallback that equity required.\" What integration failure does this reveal?","options":["A typo in the implementation plan.","An internal inconsistency: a later artifact contradicts the value decision an earlier artifact committed to, so the capstone is not coherent.","The plan is simply too short.","A missing citation in the tension map."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Capstone coherence requires later artifacts to honor the value decisions earlier ones committed to; a dropped commitment breaks integration.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_6_8_module_6_final_studio_internal_contradiction","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_module_6_final_studio","evidence_based_revision"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_8_module_6_final_studio_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ai_supported_learning","stem":"A teacher asks BigAL to review the assembled capstone before final submission. What is the defensible use of that review?","options":["Accept all of the model's edits so the capstone reads consistently.","Use the model to flag places where the four artifacts may contradict each other, then verify and resolve each flag with the teacher's own judgment.","Have the model rewrite the value decision so the artifacts agree automatically.","Ask the model to score the capstone and submit that score."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"AI is well used to surface possible inconsistencies across the capstone; resolving them is the teacher's integrative judgment.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_6_8_module_6_final_studio_ai_resolves_capstone","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_8_module_6_final_studio_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_module_6_final_studio","stem":"A capstone is highly polished but every artifact reaches a confident positive conclusion about the tool, with no tension ever left unresolved. Why might an experienced reviewer trust this less than a slightly messier capstone?","options":["Polished work is always penalized.","Uniform confidence with no surfaced tension suggests the hard tradeoffs were hidden rather than reasoned, so the judgment is harder to verify.","The messier capstone is automatically more correct.","Reviewers prefer shorter capstones regardless of content."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A capstone that never shows a hard tradeoff likely buried it; visible, reasoned tension is more credible than uniform polish.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_6_8_module_6_final_studio_polish_hides_tradeoffs","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_module_6_final_studio","evidence_based_revision"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_8_module_6_final_studio_stapled_not_integrated","pattern":"collection_mistaken_for_integration","description":"Treating a stapled set of artifacts as a capstone instead of one connected chain of judgment","trigger":"artifacts appear in sequence with no decision linking one to the next","feedback":"Show how each artifact follows from the value decision in the prior one, so the capstone reads as one argument.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_8_module_6_final_studio_internal_contradiction","pattern":"later_artifact_contradicts_earlier","description":"Letting a later capstone artifact drop or contradict a value the earlier one committed to","trigger":"implementation plan or memo violates the priority set in the tension map","feedback":"Make later artifacts honor the value decisions earlier ones committed to, or revise the decision openly.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_8_module_6_final_studio_ai_resolves_capstone","pattern":"ai_resolves_integration","description":"Delegating the integrative judgment to AI rather than using it to flag inconsistencies the teacher resolves","trigger":"learner accepts model edits that make artifacts agree without checking the underlying decision","feedback":"Use AI to flag possible contradictions, then resolve each one yourself against your value decision.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_8_module_6_final_studio_polish_hides_tradeoffs","pattern":"uniform_polish_hides_tradeoffs","description":"Producing a uniformly confident, polished capstone that hides rather than reasons through hard tradeoffs","trigger":"every artifact reaches a positive conclusion with no tension left visible","feedback":"Surface at least one hard tradeoff and show how you reasoned through it; hidden tradeoffs are not judgment.","rail_action":"practice"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"477851e584e2876ef8f82a45e34bbcb3